
# TLS certificates. Mounted read-only into nginx from ./certs on the server (docker-compose.yml).
# The directory does not exist in a development checkout -- this is here so that a private key can
# never be committed by someone who creates it locally. Nothing in this repository should ever
# contain one.
certs/

# The rest of the credential shapes, ignored tree-wide rather than per package. This is the list
# .claude/settings.json denies to the Read, Write and Edit tools, mirrored here because those tools
# are not the only route into a commit: a file dropped by a shell command, an installer or an
# external editor is git's problem alone. Unanchored on purpose -- a private key is a private key
# wherever it lands, and the two package directories are not the only places one can land.
.env*
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.cer
*.p12
*.pfx
*.p8
*.pk8
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
id_ecdsa*
credentials.json
*-service-account*.json
kubeconfig
secrets/

# Where this list deliberately stops. It covers the private-key and keystore CONTAINERS (PEM, DER's
# certificate spellings, PKCS#8 and PKCS#12 under both their extensions) and OpenSSH's three default
# private-key names, because those are the shapes that leak a secret by being committed. It does not
# chase public material (a .pub, a .csr, a certificate chain), files that live in $HOME rather than a
# repository, or names too generic to pattern without swallowing real source -- `token`, `auth`,
# `config`. A shape outside this list is a deliberate omission, not an oversight; widen it here.

# Not a key file but a config file that grows one: a registry auth token lands here the moment
# anyone runs `pnpm login` or `npm config set //registry…:_authToken`, and the file is otherwise
# unremarkable enough to be committed without a second look. No .npmrc is tracked, so nothing is
# hidden by this -- a deliberate one would need `git add -f` and a reason.
.npmrc

# The throwaway directories the gate builds and reclaims: .tmp-nginx-check (scripts/verify.sh) and
# .tmp-scope-fixtures / .tmp-hook-fixtures (scripts/selfcheck.sh). An EXIT trap does not run on
# Ctrl-C, so an interrupted run leaves one behind, and an unignored leftover counts as an untracked
# change that matches no scope arm -- the next scoped run then refuses, naming fixture files as the
# reason an image was not rebuilt. Ignoring them holds for SIGKILL too, which no trap survives, and
# it is also what lets guard-branch-bash.sh release the fixture writes on main: that exemption asks
# git whether a path is ignored and untracked (ADR-0060).
.tmp-*/

# Docker Desktop on Windows creates these when a bind-mount path is mangled by
# Git Bash MSYS path conversion (":" separators become ";C:\..."). Empty and useless.
# Prevent them by prefixing a hand-typed bind mount with MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1, per CLAUDE.md §5.
*;C
*;C/

# Personal Claude Code settings for this repo. Shared settings belong in .claude/settings.json;
# this file is the per-developer override and must never be committed.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Nested git worktrees. A parallel session may put its checkout inside this repository, and each
# one is a whole second tree -- node_modules, .next, .venv and all. Git keeps them out through
# .git/info/exclude today, which is machine-local and absent from a fresh clone, so the first
# session to create one there would see the entire checkout as untracked changes.
.claude/worktrees/

# Python bytecode. scripts/ holds plain scripts that are normally run, not imported, so this only
# fills when something imports one -- a test harness, or a REPL. Either way it is a build artifact
# and one was committed once.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Tool caches, at every level they appear: the gate runs ruff and pytest from the root, from
# scripts/ and from fl_backend/, and each working directory grows its own. Both tools also write a
# .gitignore inside the cache, which is why none of these has ever surfaced -- this does not rest
# on a tool continuing to do that.
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Audit working documents (pass reports, remediation ledger, wave reports). Local-only by design:
# the repo is public, and committing them would publish unfixed findings while they are being
# remediated. The permanent record of a finished programme lives in docs/_auditing/reports/.
# Ignored but not secret material -- CLAUDE.md §1 carves this path out, and .vscode/ with it.
docs/audit/

# Editor settings -- personal preference, and nothing in the toolchain reads them.
.vscode/
